WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:10.809
Welcome back to Curious Archive.

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:16.059
This video is a new chapter in my long-running
series exploring the incredible speculative

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.300
evolution project of Serina, the world of
birds.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.520
Serina was created by Dylan Bajda — a terrific
artist and worldbuilder — and many exciting

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.700
developments have occurred since we last explored
his creation.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.690
You may remember the fascinating premise of
the Serina project: an unknown force leaves

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:40.070
a colony of finches on the moon of Serina,
along with a few other plant and animal species

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.329
to serve as their food supply.

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:46.940
The project imagines the amazing lifeforms
that evolve from these humble beginnings over

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.900
millions of years — creatures that I’ve
explored in past videos.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:57.790
For this entry into the archive, we’ll chronicle
the next era in this evolutionary epic, and

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:03.450
witness the rise and fall of countless species
as the world of Serina continues to change.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:09.450
So, let’s return to this strange moon, and
remember to support Dylan Beta on Patreon

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.689
using the links below if you’re a fan of
this project!

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:20.499
…When we left off, the moon of Serina was
home to a teeming menagerie of incredible

00:01:20.499 --> 00:01:21.959
lifeforms.

00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:27.579
Yet as the middle Ulitmocene period wears
on, Serina has continued to cool, and in this

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:32.719
changing environment, many species are now
threatened.

00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:37.549
Somewhere on the steppe on a cold night, the
last of the flying giants looms tall above

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:38.749
the grasses.

00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:44.719
For ten million years, their species — the
Stormsonors — ruled the skies as the largest

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:47.240
flying lifeforms ever to exist.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:52.450
Now, without enough warm land to reproduce,
this Stormsonor will be the last of their

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.450
kind.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:58.969
The loss of the Stormsonors is emblematic
of how more specialized species won’t survive

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:01.570
this changing world…

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:07.770
And thus far on Serina, no species has a story
like the woodcrafters and gravediggers.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:14.430
For centuries, these two lifeforms were enemies,
predator and prey divided by a seemingly insurmountable

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.430
gap.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.130
Yet now, these two species live together in
peace — and would remain in harmony for

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.990
the next several millennia.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:27.060
In the words of the author, the triumphs and
tragedies, the joy and the sadness of ten

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.930
thousand years’ worth of lifetimes could
fill their own story.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.140
But upon Serina, where chapters are more often
measured in tens of millions of years, their

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.010
time together is just a dog-eared page in
the book of life.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.020
And this chapter too must come to a close.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:46.730
For the woodcrafters, eventually, go extinct.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:52.340
Always a small population with a highly specialized
diet, as temperatures change, they find that

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:58.970
the broad-leaf forests they depend disappear,
and soon only one woodcrafter remains.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:04.650
On nights when the skies are clear, the elderly
last woodcrafter stands on the shore of the

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.330
ocean and looks out at the horizon.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:12.690
Most nights her small companion, a chattering
sparrowgull simply called ‘Bird,’ joins

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.690
her.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.250
As a chatterer, Bird possesses the ability
to mimic the woodcrafter’s language, even

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.440
if they can’t grasp the full meaning of
the words.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.460
Yet on nights like this, when the last woodcrafter
repeats the phrase ‘I love you, Bird’

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.040
to their dear friend, Bird understands that
they are cared for.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.490
After a long life, the elderly last woodcrafter
comes to rest surrounded by her gravedigger

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:37.490
family.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:42.640
In her lifetime, she’s seen the gravediggers
achieve things she never knew were possible,

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.540
harnessing blazing lights in their hands and
shaping tools unlike anything her kind had

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:48.540
made.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.780
What’s more, the gravediggers have begun
to weave grasses into floating structures

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.180
— upon which they ride out into the sea
for days at a time.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.970
The last woodcrafter knows the gravediggers
will continue to change the world, and urges

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.480
them to explore what lies beyond the horizon.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.680
And just before the last woodcrafter closes
her eyes, she sees glimpses of a cosmic story

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.450
she can’t understand.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.790
Images of a small bird, a little fish.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.959
Moments long before her time and moments yet
to occur.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:23.790
And through it all, the last woodcrafter feels
a strange presence, who tells her that she’s

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.150
done well…

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:30.910
And then, the page turns to another chapter,
and the last woodcrafter is no more.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:36.199
Her gravedigger family lay her to rest on
the hill overlooking the sunsets she so enjoyed.

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:42.090
In an act that brings the saga of two species
to a close, this is the very last time the

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.499
gravediggers shall dig a pit for a woodcrafter.

00:04:44.499 --> 00:04:49.539
Her companion, Bird, lands upon her antler,
for a time not understanding why their friend

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:51.689
is silent.

00:04:51.689 --> 00:04:56.379
Bird stays in the gravedigger village for
a few days, watching them cast out to sea.

00:04:56.379 --> 00:05:01.090
The gravediggers are determined to fulfill
the final wishes of the woodcrafter, and chart

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:02.919
what lies beyond the horizon.

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:08.110
The last woodcrafter told them that in a world
as big as this one, there had to be more intelligent

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.590
species like themselves.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:15.370
Somewhere out upon the sea, they hope to fulfil
the dream of their dear friend.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.849
And Bird flies off on their own, beyond the
beach they know so well.

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:24.750
But they never forget the last woodcrafter,
or the phrase ‘I love you, Bird,’ which

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.430
they repeat to their children, who like Bird
do not understand the meaning, but understand

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.629
the context in which to use these words.

00:05:32.629 --> 00:05:39.099
For the chatters are, in fact, the descendants
of the last babbling jays — the first advanced

00:05:39.099 --> 00:05:43.719
intelligent life to emerge for a brief time
on Serina all those eons ago.

00:05:43.719 --> 00:05:48.460
And while chatterers aren’t as intelligent
as their ancestors from the Pangeacene, they

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.639
are a species to keep an eye on.

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:56.620
Through the coming generations, the last woodcrafter
lives on in the words the wild chatters affectionately

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:02.840
whisper to each other, carrying her memory
across the stretch of time…

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:08.390
And out to sea, the gravediggers have found
a new, brighter era has begun — The Ocean

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:09.390
Age.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:14.599
As Serina has continued to cool, the majority
of plant and animal life have become reliant

00:06:14.599 --> 00:06:19.430
upon an equatorial ocean between the massive
ice-sheets of the north and south.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:24.379
While the sea-level has technically dropped,
in this period the oceans rise to greater

00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:27.800
importance than ever before in Serina’s
history.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.789
While braving these waters, the gravediggers
will have to contend with all manner of life...

00:06:33.789 --> 00:06:37.879
Swimming near the surface are the gigantic
Oceanic Pikebirds.

00:06:37.879 --> 00:06:44.349
While they look like fish, as their name implies,
Pikebirds are, in fact, birds — specially

00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:49.729
metamorph birds, a clade which have managed
to conquer all manner of niches across Serina

00:06:49.729 --> 00:06:52.430
thanks to their highly variable biology.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:58.500
Pikebirds evolved from tadpole-like larvae
over millions of years, with the laws of hydrodynamics

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.569
and convergent evolution shaping them into
remarkably fish-like organisms.

00:07:02.569 --> 00:07:09.470
And as long as food remains abundant, these
fish birds will likely continue to flourish.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:14.169
But the seas of the Early Ocean Age aren’t
just populated by aquatic birds.

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:18.810
The Sea Dragon is a descendant of fish like
the swordtail, and is a highly successful

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.520
species — albeit the last of their particular
group.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:27.639
Able to breath atmospheric air for a short
period, the Sea Dragon has converged on a

00:07:27.639 --> 00:07:30.349
niche not dissimilar from Earth crocodiles.

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:34.750
And anyone familiar with Earth’s natural
history knows that the crocodile niche is

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:40.300
a pretty enviable long-term gig, so to speak,
as crocodiles have stuck around since the

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.249
age of the dinosaurs.

00:07:42.249 --> 00:07:47.770
And at fifteen feet, or four and a half meters,
the Sea Dragons are massive predators.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:52.369
But they’re far from the largest things
patrolling these oceans…

00:07:52.369 --> 00:07:58.110
Elsewhere upon the waves, a huge horde of
gnashing teeth and shifting legs moves upon

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.639
the surface.

00:07:59.639 --> 00:08:06.349
This is a sea shoggoth, and it’s a colony
of the most unexpected creature — the ant.

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:11.460
Left on Serina as a food source millions of
years ago, the unassuming ants have quietly

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.379
been evolving in the margins since the beginning
of this epic.

00:08:15.379 --> 00:08:22.080
By the early Ultimocene, certain ant colonies
had adapted to move in super-colonies millions-strong,

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.639
devouring everything too slow to escape their
advance through the underbrush.

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:31.469
And now, in the Ocean Age, the descendants
of these ravenous colonies have taken to the

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:34.940
sea — functioning like a single, massive
predator.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:40.710
With some Sea Shoggoths more than 30 feet,
or 9 meters across, they require a tremendous

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.630
amount of food to function.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:46.490
Once a humble food source, these ants are
now the hunters.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:49.420
Other species need to watch their backs…

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:52.090
But the social gravediggers are thriving.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:58.550
Just five thousand years after the last woodcrafter,
they’ve become a skilled, sea-faring civilization:

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.230
with traps, tools, and a written language.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:06.710
Metal-working, however, is difficult is such
an environment and has never been discovered,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:11.430
so their technological growth has remained
largely unchanged for many centuries.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:16.090
Yet in all that time, the gravediggers haven’t
forgotten the last woodcrafter’s message…

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:21.060
Through oral traditions and art like this
tablet, the memory of their lost friends has

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:22.610
been kept alive.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:27.490
The gravediggers have immortalized the woodcrafters
as parent-like figures who helped show them

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.370
the value of life different from themselves.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:35.930
As a result, modern gravedigger culture emphasizes
that gravediggers are only a small part of

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.120
a wider food web.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:42.550
And that somewhere in this ocean, there must
be other intelligent lifeforms.

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:46.670
Little do they know that deep below the water’s
surface, another advanced intelligence has

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:50.310
indeed developed in the form of the daydreamers.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:55.160
These lifeforms have been evolving, unnoticed,
under the waves since before the rise of the

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.530
woodcrafters.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:01.500
And in that time, the daydreamers developed
a way of thinking which is entirely unique.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:07.860
We’ve actually met the ancestor of the daydreamers
before in this saga in the form of seastrikers,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:12.350
a group of predators with intelligence roughly
analogous to a dolphin’s.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:17.250
The steastrikers had learned to farm and feed
upon the porplets — a type of dolfinch that

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.250
were also quite intelligent.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:25.230
And in the modern day, the daydreamers have
evolved a truly distinctive form of intelligence.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:30.370
Their brain is divided into two hemispheres
that can function independently of each other,

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:33.820
so that one half can sleep while the other
remains active.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:38.551
Strange as this might sound, the brains hemispheres
of certain dolphins can also do this to a

00:10:38.551 --> 00:10:39.890
lesser extent.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:44.580
But the experience of having a brain like
a daydreamer would be strange indeed, because

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.590
the daydreamer dreams whilst wide awake.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:53.500
Taking these dreams as visions and prophecies,
the daydreamers are a highly spiritual populace.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.740
And there is one legend that stands out from
the others.

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:02.570
A creation myth, glimpsed through shifting
dreams, of how Serina came to be: According

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:08.140
to the daydreamers, in the beginning, a mysterious
creator was alone in the void.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:13.530
To amuse themselves, they began to build,
forming something from nothing until the world

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:15.930
itself was drawn into creation.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:20.920
The creator forged the air, the water, and
the land… and all of the life that dwelled

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.310
upon the three realms.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:26.580
Then the creator gazed down from above, and
grew curious…

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:32.230
The creator wished to experience the new world,
not from the outside but from within.

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:35.890
And so, the creator fractured their essence
into thirds.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.530
One soared off into the limitless sky.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:43.740
The next walked upon the shores and far beyond
— and there they continued to reshape and

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.230
change the land as they saw fit.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:51.320
The last settled in the lowest realm, using
their strong flippers to dive down deep into

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:52.320
the seas…

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.980
And the daydreamers believe that one day,
the three splinters will find one another

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:59.850
once again, and the creator will finish its
journey…

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.920
It’s a fascinating tale.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.110
And perhaps some form of truth lies hidden
in the daydreamer’s mythos...?

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:11.780
In any case, while the story is one of unity,
a dangerous societal rift currently exists

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:15.110
between two distinct daydreamer populations.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:20.320
The undersea daydreamer populations are divided
between a group called the fishers and a group

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.350
called the pastoralists.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:27.660
The fishers feed entirely on small aquatic
life, and have a close alliance with the intelligent

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:31.430
luddies — a relative of the porplets they
once farmed.

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:37.020
The fishers are a uniquely social and curious
group, and have taken on an almost parental

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:40.330
role in keeping the luddies safe from predators.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:44.780
In contrast, the pastoralists live a very
different lifestyle.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:50.230
They continue the ancient survival strategy
of farming porplets for food, which is reflected

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:52.570
in their larger, serrated beaks.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:58.710
The porplets, however, are much changed — becoming
the nop — a livestock species almost too

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:03.080
rotund to swim and blissfully unable to comprehend
their situation.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:09.770
Indeed, the nop can only survive thanks to
the protection of their pastoralist farmhands.

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:15.160
This complicated situation, however, has led
to a seemingly endless cold war between the

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:20.380
two populations, with the fishers seeing the
pastoralists as evil, and the pastoralists

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:23.180
seeing the fishers as bizarre extremists.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:28.450
Yet now, an ancient threat is emerging that
threatens both populations…

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:32.350
For there is another, long-lost faction of
daydreamers.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:38.010
These are the Warmongers — a huge offshoot
that evolved to hunt wild prey in the distant

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:39.070
open oceans.

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:43.890
They diverged even before the fishers and
pastoralists split off.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:48.240
And now, this isolationist subset has found
their cousins again.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.780
And the warmongers have their own beliefs:
that they alone are the sole descendants of

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:58.560
a dead god… and therefore are above all
other life, including other daydreamers.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:03.230
The jaws of the Warmongers don’t distinguish
between fishers and pastoralists.

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.880
And now, an all-out war is beginning.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:10.750
But the other daydreamers have hope — hope
because the fishers believe that they have

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:14.890
found another aspect of the creator from their
most sacred myth.

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:19.620
For some time now, ever-curious fisher daydreamers
have been aware of the gravediggers, finding

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:24.410
their tools upon the ocean floor — strange
relics from another realm.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:29.320
Many fishers come to wonder if perhaps these
strange creatures are one of the lost essences

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:31.970
of their supposed creators.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:37.230
And with the warmonger threat on the rise,
the fishers have decided to, at last, make

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.360
contact with the strange creatures from the
surface.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:44.630
The first gravediggers to be greeted by a
talking whale urgently pleading for their

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.510
help are surely confused.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:51.680
And indeed, the first fishers able to mimic
the gravedigger’s slow, guttural language

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:54.130
are imperfect messengers at best.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:57.050
But the gravediggers are eager to listen.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:15:01.800
News of talking whales travels quickly, as
it is the fulfillment of the gravedigger’s

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.710
own mythos, just as it is the fishers, to
meet another intelligent species.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:11.750
And as communication improves and the danger
of the warmongers becomes clear, the gravediggers

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:16.500
realize it will take both the fishers and
pastoralists united to stop them.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:21.570
And while neither side believes an alliance
to be possible, for once they have a common

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.870
ally… and a common enemy.

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:25.890
But The Warmongers have been watching.

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:31.440
A select few know the daydreamers have allied
with mysterious animals from above the water,

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.000
and see the gravediggers as a threat to Warmonger
culture.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:39.240
After all, the advanced gravediggers seem
like living proof the daydreamer creation

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.200
story has more truth than they assumed.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:42.200
And so, the Warmongers strike first.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:47.080
With their size and strength, what chance
would the puny gravediggers have against them?

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:53.040
But indeed, by dragging the gravediggers into
the war, the Warmongers make a fatal mistake.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.550
In their next battle with the daydreamers,
fishers and pastoralists strike with poisoned

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:02.990
spears that the Warmongers can’t comprehend,
and wear stark white plates and bones that

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.200
block the force of their jaws.

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:10.080
With armor and tools created by the gravediggers,
the daydreamers are more than a match for

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:11.620
the warmonger legions.

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:15.760
But the warmongers aren’t as homogenous
as they first appear.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:20.610
They too, are made of different groups forged
from generations of struggle, and many are

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.900
only seeking to survive.

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:28.740
And as the tides of war change, young warmongers
question their allegiance to their leader.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:34.440
After much suffering, the old figures of power
are violently cast out, the warmonger ranks

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:36.000
are shattered from within.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:41.070
And the war for the ocean, at last comes to
an end…

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:46.660
Healing and reconciliation do not come immediately,
for such things are rarely so simple.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:51.529
The immediate fighting has ceased, yet the
warmonger remnants are now leaderless and

00:16:51.529 --> 00:16:55.080
disrupted, and the fishers and pastoralists
remain divided.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:59.860
Yet as years become centuries and centuries
become millennia, the varied daydreamers begin

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:03.710
to blend, guided in part by their allies from
the land.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:09.130
Together, this coastal alliance of gravediggers
and daydreamers will persist over a time scale

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.429
no culture before them has ever come close
to…

00:17:12.429 --> 00:17:17.930
And the gravediggers would never forget their
oldest allies, the woodcrafters, who helped

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:19.390
make all of this possible…

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:24.069
There are many stories yet to be told, and
questions yet to be answered.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:26.880
But that’s all I can give away for now.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:31.430
The story of ‘Serina’ is still ongoing,
and if you find the project as thought-provoking

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:37.140
as I do, please support Dylan Beta on Patreon
to gain access to entries further along in

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:38.140
Serina’s future.

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:39.680
I’ve got links in the description.

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:42.720
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:46.820
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
